  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\n- Numerous strong storms likely through daybreak this morning\n\nWe are seeing the effects of the secondary low level jet over the \narea early this morning in the form of numerous showers and storms \nover the southern 2/3rds of the forecast area. Some of the storms \nhave had some good updrafts/cores associated with them. Forecast \nsoundings show that there is an inversion from around 925mb and \nlower, which will limit the ability for stronger winds to reach the \nground. Some wind was present in storms a couple of hours ago, so \nthey will be possible. \n\nThe bigger threat looks to be the heavy rain potential. Precipitable \nwater values just short of 2.0 inches are leading to some very heavy \ndownpours. Some of the storms earlier sat over one area, and \nproduced over 2 inches in just over an hour in SW Kent County.\n\nThe expectation is that the storms over the area this morning will \npush out toward 12z or so. This lines up nicely with the low level \njet pushing east by that time, with no additional forcing moving in. \nThe front is expected to push far enough south then behind this \nmorning's storms, that as the next low level jet ramps up this \nevening, everything should stay south of the forecast area. We can't \nrule out a pop up shower/storm this afternoon with plenty of \nmoisture remaining. The coverage looks to be quite low (<20%) due to \na lack of any big feature to force it.  \n\n- Small chances of mainly diurnal showers/storms into Thursday\n\nWe get into a pattern for Tuesday through Thursday now where we will \nbe looking at some small diurnal chances for a few showers and \nstorms each afternoon and evening. Generally speaking, we should see \nMU CAPEs build to 1000-2000 J/kg each afternoon and evening with dew \npoints remaining in the 60s, and temps in the 80s. Weak short waves \nin the upper flow from the WNW will help the cause also. There may \nbe a little bit better chance across the far south where instability \nwill be a little higher due to higher dew points and warmer temps. \n\nThis includes Thursday now, where a few runs ago, it looked like \nThursday may be dry. This is due to a delay in the backdoor front \ndropping down, and clearing out the higher dew points and \ntemperatures. Now it looks like it will take until Friday before we \ncan go with a dry forecast. \n\n- Better chances of rain return next weekend\n\nThe break in the rain chances will be small for Friday, as we see a \npattern change take shape by Saturday to bring rain chances back in. \n\nThe front and the heat will be locked up to our south much of this \nweek. However, a long wave trough emerges over the Western U.S. and \nshifts the upper flow to the SW for our area. This will allow energy \nto eject from the Western trough, and lift up toward the area. The \nmodels have been hinting at this quite consistently, just with some \nfluctuations in track and timing. \n\nThe stronger system will tend to draw better moisture northward, and \nthis will be possible as early as early as Friday night into \nSaturday at the leading edge of the moisture advection. The system \nwill eventually move through, and push a front through that will \nsweep out the moisture and warmer temperatures.",
